Dear INLA group,<div><br></div><div>I meet a question when I learn using INLA to fit a CAR model in space-time setting.</div><div><br></div><div>The following code is copied from the INLA book &quot;Spatial and Spatio-temporal</div><div>Bayesian Models with R-INLA&quot; </div><div><br></div><div>formula.par&lt;- y ~ 1 + f(county,  model=&quot;


besag&quot;, graph=Ohio.adj,<br>constr=TRUE),<br>+ f(county1, year, model=&quot;iid&quot;, constr=TRUE),<br>+ year 
<br style="font-variant-numeric:normal;font-variant-east-asian:normal;font-variant-alternates:normal;line-height:normal;text-align:-webkit-auto"></div><div><br></div><div>The data contains both space (i=88) and time (t=21) dimnsions, that is to say, we have 88*21 observations.</div><div><br></div><div>This piece of code &quot;f(county,  model=&quot; besag&quot;, graph=Ohio.adj, constr=TRUE)&quot; is used to hand spatial effects. The index is based on the &quot;country&quot;, that is 1...88. However, we have 88*21 observations.</div><div><br></div><div>How did you extend this term for 1...1848 observations? Where did you put the  kronecker product? Or you extend this term through the group argument internally?</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for your help</div><div>Charles LING</div>
